WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.850
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.500
I consider this one to be one of the most
influential papers in the field of light transport.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.020
Normally, to create a photorealistic image,
we have to create a digital copy of a scene,

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.770
and simulate the paths of millions of light
rays between the camera and the light sources.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:27.150
This is a very laborious task that usually
takes from minutes to hours on a complex scene,

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:32.070
noting that there are many well-known corner
cases that can take up to days as well.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:37.430
As the rays of light can bounce around potentially
indefinitely, and if we add that realistic

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:43.210
materials and detailed scene geometry descriptions
are not easy to handle mathematically, it

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:47.430
is easy to see why this is a notoriously difficult
problem.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:52.000
Simulating light transport in real time has
been an enduring problem and is still not

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.110
solved completely for every possible material
model and light transport effect.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:02.580
However, Voxel Cone Tracing is as good of
a solution as one can wish for at the moment.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:07.470
The original formulation of the problem is
continuous, which means that rays of light

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.820
can bounce around in infinitely many directions
and the entirety of this digital world is

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.010
considered to be a continuum.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:19.720
If we look at the mathematical formulation,
we see infinities everywhere we look.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:23.560
If you would like to learn more about this,
I am holding a Master-level course at the

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.380
Technical University of Vienna, the entirety
of which is available on YouTube.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.670
As always, the link is available in the video
description for the more curious Fellow Scholars

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.670
out there.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:39.560
If we try to approximate this continuous representation
with tiny tiny cubes, we get a version of

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.689
the problem that is much less complex, and
easier to tackle.

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:49.030
If we do this well, we can make it adaptive,
which means that these cubes are smaller where

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:53.010
there is a lot of information so we don't
lose out on many details.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:57.260
This data structure we call a sparse voxel
octree.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:02.240
For such a solution, mathematicians like to
say that this technique works on a discretized

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.540
version of the continuous problem.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.479
And since we're solving a vastly simplified
version of the problem, the question is always

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:13.510
whether this way we can remain true to the
original solution.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:18.870
And the results show beauty unlike anything
we've seen in computer game graphics.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.120
Just look at this absolutely amazing footage.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.800
Ice cream for my eyes.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.299
And all this runs in real time on your consumer
graphics card.

00:02:28.299 --> 00:02:32.480
Imagine this in the virtual reality applications
of the future.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.480
My goodness.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.040
I've chosen the absolute best profession.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.040
Also, this technique maps really well to the
graphical card and is already implemented

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:47.109
in Unreal Engine 4 and NVIDIA has a framework,
GameWorks, where they are experimenting with

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:49.430
this in their project by the name VXGI.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:56.090
I had a very pleasant visit at NVIDIA's GameWorks
lab in Switzerland not so long ago, friendly

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.389
greetings to all the great and fun people
in the team!

00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:01.829
Some kinks still have to be worked out.

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:06.489
For instance, there are still issues with
light leaking through thin objects.

00:03:06.489 --> 00:03:12.230
Beyond that, the implementation of this algorithm
contains a multitude of tiny little distinct

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.230
elements.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:18.139
It is indeed true that many of the elements
are puzzle pieces that are interchangeable

00:03:18.139 --> 00:03:22.730
and can be implemented in a number of different
ways, and that's likely one of the reasons

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:27.950
why NVIDIA and others are still preparing
their implementation for widespread industry

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:28.950
use.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:34.120
Soon, we'll be able to solidify the details
some more and see what the best practices

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.120
are.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.069
I cannot wait to see this technique appear
in the video games of the future.

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:44.730
Note that this, and future episodes will be
available in 4K resolution for a significant

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.199
bump in the visual quality of the series.

00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:51.890
It takes a ton of resources to produce these
videos, but it's now possible through the

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:54.580
support of you Fellow Scholars on Patreon.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.919
There is a more detailed write-up on that,
I've included it in the video description.

00:03:58.919 --> 00:04:04.150
Thank you so much for supporting the show
throughout 2016, and looking forward to continuing

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:07.120
our journey together in 2017!

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.820
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

